1
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
In a town like Twin Peaks, no one is innocent.

2
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:06,000
Twin Peaks: Fire Walk with Me is a drama, mystery,
horror film directed by David Lynch.

3
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:36,000
In the questionable town of Deer Meadow, Washington, FBI Agent Desmond
inexplicably disappears while hunting for the man who murdered a teen girl.

4
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:06,000
The killer is never apprehended, and, after experiencing dark visions and supernatural encounters,
Agent Dale Cooper chillingly predicts that the culprit will claim another life.

5
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:36,000
Meanwhile, in the more cozy town of Twin Peaks, hedonistic beauty Laura
Palmer hangs with lowlifes and seems destined for a grisly fate.

6
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:06,000
Laura Palmer, played by Sheryl Lee.

7
00:31:30,000 --> 00:31:36,000
Leland Palmer, played by Ray Wise.

8
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:06,000
Shelly Johnson, played by Mädchen Amick.

9
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:36,000
Bobby Briggs, played by Dana Ashbrook.

10
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
Ronette Pulaski, played by Phoebe Augustine.

11
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:36,000
Phillip Jeffries, played by David Bowie.

12
00:54:00,000 --> 00:54:06,000
Twin Peaks: Fire Walk with Me is a 1992 psychological horror film directed
by David Lynch, and co-written by Lynch and Robert Engels.

13
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:36,000
It serves as a prequel to seasons one and two of the television series Twin
Peaks (1990–1991), created and produced by Mark Frost and Lynch.

14
01:03:00,000 --> 01:03:06,000
It begins with the FBI's investigation into the murder of Teresa Banks (Pamela Gidley) before shifting to the last seven days of the life
of Laura Palmer (Sheryl Lee), a popular-but-troubled high school student in the fictional town of Twin Peaks, Washington.

15
01:07:30,000 --> 01:07:36,000
Palmer's murder was the primary plot thread of the original TV series.

16
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
Greenlit shortly after the TV series was cancelled, Fire Walk with Me has a much darker tone than the TV series
and does not address many of season two's unfinished narratives, including its cliffhanger ending.

17
01:16:30,000 --> 01:16:36,000
Although most of the television cast reprised their roles for the film, many
comparatively lighthearted scenes featuring town residents were cut.

18
01:21:00,000 --> 01:21:06,000
In addition, the series' main star, Kyle MacLachlan (Dale Cooper), asked for his role to be
downsized, and Lara Flynn Boyle's character Donna Hayward was recast with Moira Kelly.

19
01:25:30,000 --> 01:25:36,000
In 2014, several deleted scenes were recut and
released as Twin Peaks: The Missing Pieces.

20
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Fire Walk with Me premiered at the 1992 Cannes Film
Festival in competition for the Palme d'Or.

21
01:34:30,000 --> 01:34:36,000
The film was notoriously polarizing: Lynch said that the film was booed at
Cannes, and the American press generally panned the film.

22
01:39:00,000 --> 01:39:06,000
The film was controversial for its frank and vivid depiction of sexual abuse, its
relative absence of fan-favorite characters, and its surrealistic style.

23
01:43:30,000 --> 01:43:36,000
It was a box-office bomb in North America, but fared better in Japan and France.

24
01:48:00,000 --> 01:48:06,000
Due to the poor reception, plans for a sequel were abandoned.

25
01:52:30,000 --> 01:52:36,000
The film has been positively re-evaluated in the 21st century, and is
now widely regarded as one of Lynch's major works.

26
01:57:00,000 --> 01:57:06,000
Lynch and Frost eventually received funding to produce a third season of the TV
series in 2017, which revisited several plot threads from the film.

27
02:01:30,000 --> 02:01:36,000
In 2019, the British Film Institute ranked Fire Walk
with Me the fourth-best film of the 1990s.

28
02:06:00,000 --> 02:06:06,000
The film explores themes of high school, small town,
double life, sexual abuse, detective.

29
02:10:30,000 --> 02:10:36,000
The tone is dark and emotional and mind_bending.
